access_output/file: skip empty blocks instead of failing on them

Write(), used for files opened for writing, treats write() returning 0 as
an error. A zero-length block makes write() return 0, so the block chain is
dropped and "cannot write: <stale errno>" is logged, often "Connection timed
out". Transcoding audio to WAV hands the file output such blocks and floods
the log, although the output is whole. The other write path in this file
already skips empty blocks.

diff --git i/modules/access_output/file.c w/modules/access_output/file.c
index 45460b225d..07e26b0e11 100644
--- i/modules/access_output/file.c
+++ w/modules/access_output/file.c
@@ -82,6 +82,14 @@ static ssize_t Write( sout_access_out_t *p_access, block_t *p_buffer )
 
     while( p_buffer )
     {
+        if (p_buffer->i_buffer == 0)
+        {   /* write() of nothing returns 0, which is not an error */
+            block_t *p_next = p_buffer->p_next;
+            block_Release (p_buffer);
+            p_buffer = p_next;
+            continue;
+        }
+
         ssize_t val = write(fd, p_buffer->p_buffer, p_buffer->i_buffer);
         if (val <= 0)
         {
